The compile enforcement is a type-system story worth one layer down: chan<- T and <-chan T are distinct types, and the close operation is defined only on chan<- T (and the bidirectional chan T). When you convert a bidirectional channel to a receive-only view, the runtime value is unchanged — the same pointer — but the compile-time type no longer HAS the close method. The compiler rejects the call before any runtime question arises.

It's the same narrow-by-conversion trick as the send/receive restrictions, applied to lifecycle: the direction type says what you can DO, and close is an action reserved to the direction that owns the channel's end-of-life. Note the asymmetry with send: a <-chan can't send (compile) and can't close (compile) — the receive-only view is purely a consuming view. The chan<- (send-only) CAN close — the sender owns the end — and that's the signature of the canonical producer:

```go
func produce(out chan<- int) {
    defer close(out)  // legal on chan<-, idiomatic at the producer
    for _, v := range data {
        out <- v
    }
}
```

defer close(out) at the top of a producer is the lifecycle idiom — no matter how the function returns, the close fires, every receiver's range terminates, and the pipeline drains cleanly. That one line is the ownership model in miniature: the function that has the send authority exercises the close authority at the same scope.

Channels have no drop mode. The three possible outcomes of a send, exhaustively:

1. The value is delivered (unbuffered: a receiver took it; buffered: a slot holds it) — possibly after blocking.
2. The send blocks because the channel is full or nil — the sender waits.
3. The channel is closed — panic.

There is no fourth outcome where the value vanishes. That guarantee is what makes channels correct-by-construction for handoffs: a completed send is a delivered value, full stop.

But real systems need dropping — a metrics sink that shouldn't block the app when the collector is slow, a UI event queue that discards stale clicks. Go's answer is you build the drop with select:

```go
select {
case ch <- v:
    // delivered
 default:
    // channel full — drop, log, count, never block the caller
}
```

The default case makes the select non-blocking: if no case is ready immediately, default runs. This is the idiom for bounded-queue-with-drop semantics on top of a buffered channel.

The time-bounded variant — a send that waits up to a deadline then gives up:

```go
select {
case ch <- v:
    // delivered in time
case <-time.After(100 * time.Millisecond):
    // give up — timeout path
}
```

The interview point: the primitive is strict (block or panic), and the policy (drop, timeout, retry) is yours, built from select. Go keeps the channel honest — it will never silently lose data — and puts the lossy decisions where they belong: in explicit, visible application code.
